          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Family and Succession</strong><br>\nHe had over a hundred children with his many wives and consorts, which ensured a long line of succession. His most beloved wife, Queen Nefertari, held a special place in his heart and was immortalized in many monuments and texts.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nRamses II died around 1213 BCE, after ruling for nearly 67 years, one of the longest reigns in Egyptian history. He left behind a legacy of architectural and cultural achievements. His mummy was discovered in the Valley of the Kings and is a testament to the sophisticated mummification techniques of ancient Egypt.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ramses II in Modern Times</strong><br>\nThe name Ramses II remains synonymous with greatness in modern times. His rule represents the zenith of Egyptian artistic and architectural endeavors. Today, he is a symbol of ancient Egypt's glory and his statues, temples, and relics continue to attract tourists from around the world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
